::ALG2_U05_L03_Q01::
Question ID: ALG2_U05_L03_Q01
MLA Standard: MLA.A2.SYS.4
Question:
Solve y=x² and y=9.
{
=A. (-3,9) and (3,9)#Correct. Set x²=9.
~B. (9,81)#This is on the parabola but not y=9.
~C. (0,9)#This is not on the parabola.
~D. (3,3)#This is not on y=9.
}


::ALG2_U05_L03_Q02::
Question ID: ALG2_U05_L03_Q02
MLA Standard: MLA.A2.SYS.4
Question:
Solve y=x² and y=x+6.
{
~A. (2,8)#This is on the line but not the parabola.
=B. (-2,4) and (3,9)#Correct. Set x²=x+6.
~C. (0,6)#This is on the line but not the parabola.
~D. No solution#The equations intersect twice.
}


::ALG2_U05_L03_Q03::
Question ID: ALG2_U05_L03_Q03
MLA Standard: MLA.A2.SYS.4
Question:
Solve y=x²-1 and y=3.
{
~A. (3,8)#This is not on y=3.
~B. (0,3)#This is not on the parabola.
=C. (-2,3) and (2,3)#Correct. Set x²-1=3.
~D. (2,2)#This is not on y=3.
}


::ALG2_U05_L03_Q04::
Question ID: ALG2_U05_L03_Q04
MLA Standard: MLA.A2.SYS.4
Question:
Solve y=x² and y=-x+2.
{
~A. (2,4) and (-1,1)#These use sign errors.
~B. (0,2) only#This is on the line but not the parabola.
~C. No solution#The graphs intersect twice.
=D. (-2,4) and (1,1)#Correct. Set x²=-x+2.
}


::ALG2_U05_L03_Q05::
Question ID: ALG2_U05_L03_Q05
MLA Standard: MLA.A2.SYS.4
Question:
Which solution is invalid if x represents a number of items?
{
=A. x=-4#Correct. A negative number of items is not meaningful.
~B. x=0#Zero items can be meaningful.
~C. x=2#Positive items can be meaningful.
~D. x=10#Positive items can be meaningful.
}


::ALG2_U05_L03_Q06::
Question ID: ALG2_U05_L03_Q06
MLA Standard: MLA.A2.SYS.4
Question:
What should be checked after solving a constrained system?
{
~A. Only one equation#Every equation matters.
=B. Each equation and each context restriction#Correct. A feasible solution must satisfy both algebra and context.
~C. Only the graph shape#The actual point must be checked.
~D. Only the largest x-value#All candidate points require checking.
}


::ALG2_U05_L03_Q07::
Question ID: ALG2_U05_L03_Q07
MLA Standard: MLA.A2.SYS.4
Question:
A line and circle can have how many intersection points?
{
~A. Always 1#Different positions are possible.
~B. Always 2#A tangent line has one intersection.
=C. 0, 1, or 2#Correct. A line can miss, touch, or cross a circle twice.
~D. Always 0#Intersections may occur.
}


::ALG2_U05_L03_Q08::
Question ID: ALG2_U05_L03_Q08
MLA Standard: MLA.A2.SYS.4
Question:
A parabola and horizontal line can intersect how many times?
{
~A. Always 1#This ignores cases where the horizontal line misses the parabola or crosses it twice.
~B. Always 2#This ignores the tangent case, where the horizontal line touches the parabola once.
~C. Exactly 3#A horizontal line and parabola cannot intersect three times.
=D. 0, 1, or 2#Correct. The line can miss, touch, or cross the parabola twice.
}


::ALG2_U05_L03_Q09::
Question ID: ALG2_U05_L03_Q09
MLA Standard: MLA.A2.SYS.4
Question:
Which point satisfies y=x² and y=5x-6?
{
=A. (2,4) and (3,9)#Correct. Set x²=5x-6.
~B. (1,-1)#This is on the line but not the parabola.
~C. (6,36)#This is on the parabola but not the line.
~D. No solution#The equations intersect twice.
}


::ALG2_U05_L03_Q10::
Question ID: ALG2_U05_L03_Q10
MLA Standard: MLA.A2.SYS.4
Question:
Solve y=x²+2 and y=6.
{
~A. (6,38)#This is not on y=6.
=B. (-2,6) and (2,6)#Correct. Set x²+2=6.
~C. (0,6)#This is not on the parabola.
~D. (2,2)#This is not on y=6.
}


::ALG2_U05_L03_Q11::
Question ID: ALG2_U05_L03_Q11
MLA Standard: MLA.A2.SYS.4
Question:
In a context, why might a mathematically correct solution be rejected?
{
~A. It has two coordinates.#Ordered pairs are expected.
~B. It is an intersection.#Intersections are usually candidate solutions.
=C. It violates a constraint such as nonnegative time.#Correct. Context restrictions can limit valid solutions.
~D. It was found algebraically.#Algebraic solutions can be valid after checking.
}


::ALG2_U05_L03_Q12::
Question ID: ALG2_U05_L03_Q12
MLA Standard: MLA.A2.SYS.4
Question:
Which point satisfies y=x²-4 and y=0?
{
~A. (0,-4)#This is on the parabola but not y=0.
~B. (4,0)#This does not satisfy the parabola.
~C. No solution#The graph crosses y=0 twice.
=D. (-2,0) and (2,0)#Correct. Set x²-4=0.
}


::ALG2_U05_L03_Q13::
Question ID: ALG2_U05_L03_Q13
MLA Standard: MLA.A2.SYS.4
Question:
What does feasible mean in a constrained nonlinear system?
{
=A. Satisfies all equations, inequalities, and context limits#Correct. Feasible means all conditions are true.
~B. Satisfies one equation only#All conditions matter.
~C. Has the greatest y-value#Feasible does not mean maximum.
~D. Lies on the x-axis#Feasible points can be elsewhere.
}


::ALG2_U05_L03_Q14::
Question ID: ALG2_U05_L03_Q14
MLA Standard: MLA.A2.SYS.4
Question:
Solve y=x² and y=2x+3.
{
~A. (1,5)#This is on the line but not the parabola.
=B. (-1,1) and (3,9)#Correct. Set x²=2x+3.
~C. (0,3)#This is not on the parabola.
~D. No solution#The graphs intersect twice.
}


::ALG2_U05_L03_Q15::
Question ID: ALG2_U05_L03_Q15
MLA Standard: MLA.A2.SYS.4
Question:
Which is a solution of y=x² and y=16?
{
~A. (16,4)#This reverses coordinates.
~B. (0,16)#This is not on the parabola.
=C. (4,16)#Correct. 4²=16 and y=16.
~D. (4,4)#This is not on y=16.
}


::ALG2_U05_L03_Q16::
Question ID: ALG2_U05_L03_Q16
MLA Standard: MLA.A2.SYS.4
Question:
Why can nonlinear systems have two solutions?
{
~A. They always include two equations.#Two equations can still have zero or one solution.
~B. They always have no solution.#Intersections can exist.
~C. Because x and y are both variables.#Variables alone do not determine solution count.
=D. Curved graphs can intersect another graph more than once.#Correct. Nonlinear shapes can cross a line or curve at multiple points.
}


::ALG2_U05_L03_Q17::
Question ID: ALG2_U05_L03_Q17
MLA Standard: MLA.A2.SYS.4
Question:
Solve y=x² and y=-4.
{
=A. No real solution#Correct. A square cannot equal a negative output on y=x².
~B. (2,-4)#This is not on y=x².
~C. (-2,-4)#This is not on y=x².
~D. (0,-4)#This is not on y=x².
}


::ALG2_U05_L03_Q18::
Question ID: ALG2_U05_L03_Q18
MLA Standard: MLA.A2.SYS.4
Question:
Which point satisfies y=x²+1 and y=5?
{
~A. (5,26)#This is not on y=5.
=B. (-2,5) and (2,5)#Correct. Set x²+1=5.
~C. (0,5)#This is not on the curve.
~D. (2,2)#This is not on y=5.
}


::ALG2_U05_L03_Q19::
Question ID: ALG2_U05_L03_Q19
MLA Standard: MLA.A2.SYS.4
Question:
What is the graph meaning of no solution?
{
~A. The graphs are identical.#Identical graphs have many solutions.
~B. Every point works.#That means infinitely many solutions.
=C. The graphs do not intersect in the feasible region.#Correct. No shared feasible point exists.
~D. The y-intercepts are equal.#Equal y-intercepts could be one solution.
}


::ALG2_U05_L03_Q20::
Question ID: ALG2_U05_L03_Q20
MLA Standard: MLA.A2.SYS.4
Question:
If a solution is (4,10), what should be true?
{
~A. Only x=4 must work.#The y-coordinate matters too.
~B. Only y=10 must work.#The x-coordinate matters too.
~C. It only needs to be near an intersection.#A solution must satisfy exactly.
=D. Substituting x=4 and y=10 makes every relation true.#Correct. Both coordinates must satisfy all relations.
}

